WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.505
OK, this is the thing.

00:00:21.505 --> 00:00:25.809
We've done a lot of
work on ionic bonds.

00:00:25.809 --> 00:00:29.680
And I drew the ionic bond
energy again, on Monday.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.448
And remember, in
an ironic bond--

00:00:32.448 --> 00:00:34.785
so let's take sodium chloride--

00:00:34.785 --> 00:00:37.688
we have, oh, we're going
to go Lewis, right?

00:00:41.859 --> 00:00:44.261
And people say, well, why
did I draw that dot there.

00:00:44.261 --> 00:00:46.463
I could have drawn two dots
there and that dot there.

00:00:46.463 --> 00:00:47.097
Sure.

00:00:47.097 --> 00:00:47.598
Yeah.

00:00:47.598 --> 00:00:49.500
But they're kind of
looking at each other.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:50.401
So, you know.

00:00:50.401 --> 00:00:50.901
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:55.706
And remember in an iconic bond
chlorine is like, give me!

00:00:55.706 --> 00:00:56.707
I want!

00:00:56.707 --> 00:00:59.676
And that's the kind of
relationship they have, right?

00:00:59.676 --> 00:01:02.546
And so this went into
something like this, where

00:01:02.546 --> 00:01:04.715
sodium was like, OK, fine.

00:01:04.715 --> 00:01:05.215
Take it.

00:01:05.215 --> 00:01:06.417
I'm a plus now.

00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:08.385
And chlorine was like,
thank you very much.

00:01:08.385 --> 00:01:12.689
I'm fully octeted, right?

00:01:12.689 --> 00:01:13.590
Like that.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:14.958
And that's the ionic bond.

00:01:14.958 --> 00:01:16.560
So this is ionic.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.861
And we learned this.

00:01:17.861 --> 00:01:20.631
OK, but see, there is another
kind of bond that can happen.

00:01:20.631 --> 00:01:24.201
It's not all about
taking and one per-- no.

00:01:24.201 --> 00:01:27.137
It can also be about sharing.

00:01:27.137 --> 00:01:29.807
About sharing.

00:01:29.807 --> 00:01:31.742
So if I have hydrogen
instead, let's

00:01:31.742 --> 00:01:33.644
suppose I have
hydrogen like up there.

00:01:33.644 --> 00:01:35.979
And I've got hydrogen now.

00:01:35.979 --> 00:01:38.916
And here is hydrogen.
Well, see when

00:01:38.916 --> 00:01:42.152
they see-- geshundheit-- each
other, a very different thing

00:01:42.152 --> 00:01:43.620
happens.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.057
Because one of the hydrogens
doesn't say, gimme.

00:01:47.057 --> 00:01:48.991
And the other one
doesn't say, sure.

00:01:48.991 --> 00:01:51.228
Well, but something
else happens.

00:01:51.228 --> 00:01:52.996
Because they get closer.

00:01:55.933 --> 00:01:58.402
They get closer, and closer.

00:01:58.402 --> 00:01:59.169
Oh!

00:01:59.169 --> 00:02:02.005
Let's look at this in terms
of like, OK, so here's

00:02:02.005 --> 00:02:04.174
a plus and minus.

00:02:04.174 --> 00:02:05.843
Oh, yeah.

00:02:05.843 --> 00:02:08.612
It's not an orbit,
it's an orbital!

00:02:08.612 --> 00:02:11.615
And here's another plus,
and here's another minus.

00:02:11.615 --> 00:02:13.917
And these electrons--
you know, the thing is,

00:02:13.917 --> 00:02:17.554
the electrons are like, I
don't like you other electron.

00:02:17.554 --> 00:02:18.956
You're repelling me.

00:02:18.956 --> 00:02:22.091
And the protons are like, I
don't like you other proton.

00:02:22.091 --> 00:02:23.827
You're repelling me.

00:02:23.827 --> 00:02:25.896
But the electrons
do like protons.

00:02:25.896 --> 00:02:31.034
And at a certain point, if
you bring these together,

00:02:31.034 --> 00:02:34.271
one of them is like,
hey, I got an idea!

00:02:34.271 --> 00:02:37.841
What if I'm attracted to
you sometimes, over here,

00:02:37.841 --> 00:02:39.442
and I'm this electron.

00:02:39.442 --> 00:02:42.079
I'm like, I want to
be attracted to you.

00:02:42.079 --> 00:02:45.449
And I'll tell you what, you
can be attracted to my proton,

00:02:45.449 --> 00:02:46.817
and we'll share.

00:02:46.817 --> 00:02:49.119
We'll share.

00:02:49.119 --> 00:02:51.522
And so, when these
come together,

00:02:51.522 --> 00:02:58.028
what you get is the protons
are a certain distance apart,

00:02:58.028 --> 00:03:00.964
that's set by how far you
can push them in without them

00:03:00.964 --> 00:03:03.000
feeling that repulsion.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.135
And the electrons-- as long
as they don't get like,

00:03:05.135 --> 00:03:06.737
you know super on
top of each other,

00:03:06.737 --> 00:03:08.505
because they're going
to repel each other--

00:03:08.505 --> 00:03:09.873
they're happier.

00:03:09.873 --> 00:03:12.309
They're happier because they
got two positive charges

00:03:12.309 --> 00:03:15.746
that they can kind of
go around and be near.

00:03:15.746 --> 00:03:21.084
That leads to happiness--
lower energy bond!

00:03:21.084 --> 00:03:24.121
But you can see right now,
this is a very different kind

00:03:24.121 --> 00:03:25.622
of bond.

00:03:25.622 --> 00:03:26.123
Right?

00:03:26.123 --> 00:03:28.492
This is not ionic.

00:03:28.492 --> 00:03:29.860
This is covalent.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.161
This is covalent.

00:03:31.161 --> 00:03:37.935
When share electrons
it's a covalent bond.

00:03:37.935 --> 00:03:38.602
OK?

00:03:38.602 --> 00:03:43.106
And that is something
we write differently.

00:03:43.106 --> 00:03:45.542
We write it with a dash.

00:03:45.542 --> 00:03:48.111
Well, or you could've
also written it--

00:03:48.111 --> 00:03:50.914
or maybe you should say, well,
let's just put those electrons

00:03:50.914 --> 00:03:54.017
in between them like that.

00:03:54.017 --> 00:03:54.518
Right?

00:03:54.518 --> 00:03:56.720
And it's showing that
those electrons didn't

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.256
go to one or the other,
they're in between.

00:03:59.256 --> 00:04:02.492
They are what are making
up the bond together,

00:04:02.492 --> 00:04:06.096
sharing those protons
across the atoms.

00:04:06.096 --> 00:04:06.597
OK.

00:04:06.597 --> 00:04:08.465
So that's nice.

00:04:08.465 --> 00:04:11.602
That's hydrogen.
What happens when

00:04:11.602 --> 00:04:15.706
we get to something a little
bit more complicated, right?

00:04:15.706 --> 00:04:19.009
So let's go to water.

00:04:19.009 --> 00:04:21.245
Now, if I have
something like water--

00:04:21.245 --> 00:04:24.982
so now, I'm going to go
through some examples.

00:04:24.982 --> 00:04:26.082
Let's suppose I have water.

00:04:26.082 --> 00:04:28.919
Well, I've got this here,
and I've got this here.

00:04:28.919 --> 00:04:30.554
And now, remember,
we're going to think

00:04:30.554 --> 00:04:32.856
about things in
terms of Lewis dots,

00:04:32.856 --> 00:04:35.692
which I introduced
to you on Monday.

00:04:35.692 --> 00:04:39.329
Those are the valence electrons
in an atom, which are what

00:04:39.329 --> 00:04:41.031
do all of the chemical bonding.

00:04:41.031 --> 00:04:42.799
So those are the ones
we're going to carry.

00:04:42.799 --> 00:04:46.236
Now, how many dots do I
have for oxygen. I forget.

00:04:46.236 --> 00:04:47.070
Six!

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:47.738
Six.

00:04:47.738 --> 00:04:51.008
And if you didn't know,
you know where to look.

00:04:51.008 --> 00:04:54.177
Because you're always carrying
your periodic table with you--

00:04:54.177 --> 00:04:55.178
always!

00:04:55.178 --> 00:04:58.715
And so I might
write six like that.

00:04:58.715 --> 00:04:59.249
OK.

00:04:59.249 --> 00:05:02.786
What lines do I draw where?

00:05:02.786 --> 00:05:05.122
How are these shared?

00:05:05.122 --> 00:05:17.200
So how are the electrons
shared in covalent bonds?

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.870
Covalent bonds.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:27.110
And that is where
Lewis will help.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:28.612
That is where Lewis will help.

00:05:28.612 --> 00:05:33.183
How do I take these valence
configurations of electrons

00:05:33.183 --> 00:05:38.722
and make a molecule with these
kinds of sharing bonds in it?

00:05:38.722 --> 00:05:42.826
And there's actually
a fairly simple recipe

00:05:42.826 --> 00:05:44.728
that I want to teach you
today, because that's

00:05:44.728 --> 00:05:46.063
how we're going to do it.

00:05:46.063 --> 00:05:47.731
And the recipe is written here.

00:05:47.731 --> 00:05:49.566
And don't worry, there's
a lot of text here,

00:05:49.566 --> 00:05:53.170
but I'm going to go through it
for three different examples.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:56.373
And if you haven't done Lewis--

00:05:56.373 --> 00:05:58.909
I know some of you have in high
school, some of you have not.

00:05:58.909 --> 00:05:59.743
That's OK.

00:05:59.743 --> 00:06:01.978
If you haven't,
practice is really key.

00:06:01.978 --> 00:06:02.946
All right?

00:06:02.946 --> 00:06:05.916
We'll do three molecules,
maybe four, today.

00:06:05.916 --> 00:06:09.252
And I encourage you to do
a bunch more as practice.

00:06:09.252 --> 00:06:13.023
And this is the recipe for
drawing Lewis structures.

00:06:13.023 --> 00:06:16.760
And Lewis structures is
what tells us how electrons

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:21.131
are bonded in these molecules.

00:06:21.131 --> 00:06:24.735
And so, let's follow this
for the one I have up here.

00:06:24.735 --> 00:06:27.337
Let's follow this for water.

00:06:27.337 --> 00:06:27.838
OK?

00:06:27.838 --> 00:06:29.706
So we're going to go
through this recipe.

00:06:29.706 --> 00:06:32.909
Now, the first--
so, one through six.

00:06:32.909 --> 00:06:35.679
I will write one through
six for the example.

00:06:35.679 --> 00:06:37.547
I will parallel that recipe.

00:06:37.547 --> 00:06:40.217
So first, connect atom central.

00:06:40.217 --> 00:06:43.754
Central atom is often the
least electronegative-- already

00:06:43.754 --> 00:06:45.021
I'm stuck!

00:06:45.021 --> 00:06:46.456
Because I didn't
tell you anything

00:06:46.456 --> 00:06:47.957
about electronegativity.

00:06:47.957 --> 00:06:49.793
That's coming a little later.

00:06:49.793 --> 00:06:53.330
Today, I will tell you
what electronegativity is.

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:55.165
For now, trust me.

00:06:55.165 --> 00:06:56.700
So the first step
is connect atoms.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:01.204
Central atom is often the
least electronegative.

00:07:01.204 --> 00:07:01.705
OK.

00:07:01.705 --> 00:07:02.472
Well, so let's see.

00:07:02.472 --> 00:07:11.214
So for water, so step one, the H
atoms are going to be terminal.

00:07:11.214 --> 00:07:13.950
That means that
they're on the outside.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:14.885
Terminal.

00:07:14.885 --> 00:07:15.385
OK?

00:07:15.385 --> 00:07:16.286
Outside.

00:07:16.286 --> 00:07:19.756
They're also called terminal.

00:07:19.756 --> 00:07:25.996
And so, the arrangement should
be something like H, O, and H.

00:07:25.996 --> 00:07:26.763
OK, good.

00:07:26.763 --> 00:07:28.532
So that's going to
be my arrangement.

00:07:28.532 --> 00:07:29.032
OK.

00:07:29.032 --> 00:07:31.802
Step two-- determine the total
number of valence electrons.

00:07:31.802 --> 00:07:33.103
OK, I can do that.

00:07:33.103 --> 00:07:34.538
So two.

00:07:34.538 --> 00:07:36.606
Well, I've got
one, two , three--

00:07:36.606 --> 00:07:37.107
OK.

00:07:37.107 --> 00:07:40.610
I've got eight
valence electrons.

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:41.945
Good.

00:07:41.945 --> 00:07:42.446
OK.

00:07:42.446 --> 00:07:47.350
Step three is where
it gets really fun.

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:50.487
Place bonding pair of electrons.

00:07:50.487 --> 00:07:52.155
Go all in, just like I did for--

00:07:52.155 --> 00:07:54.124
So there's a bonding
pair right there.

00:07:54.124 --> 00:07:55.258
There they are.

00:07:55.258 --> 00:07:57.260
Two electrons in a bonding pair.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:02.165
So place bonding pair
between adjacent atoms.

00:08:02.165 --> 00:08:02.632
OK.

00:08:02.632 --> 00:08:03.300
I can do that.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:04.468
So let's see.

00:08:04.468 --> 00:08:05.836
Here we go.

00:08:05.836 --> 00:08:10.674
H, O, H. Right?

00:08:10.674 --> 00:08:11.608
OK.

00:08:11.608 --> 00:08:13.210
So there's my bonding pair.

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:14.478
I put two electrons.

00:08:14.478 --> 00:08:19.483
But now, you see, OK, I only
used up four electrons there.

00:08:19.483 --> 00:08:19.983
OK?

00:08:19.983 --> 00:08:24.221
Now, starting with that,
now add enough electrons

00:08:24.221 --> 00:08:28.892
to each one to form an octet.

00:08:28.892 --> 00:08:32.028
Start with terminal atoms,
add enough electrons

00:08:32.028 --> 00:08:33.496
to form an octet.

00:08:33.496 --> 00:08:34.063
OK?

00:08:34.063 --> 00:08:34.698
So let's see.

00:08:34.698 --> 00:08:38.835
But hydrogen is special, because
hydrogen's octet is just two.

00:08:38.835 --> 00:08:40.604
Hydrogen doesn't want eight.

00:08:40.604 --> 00:08:42.371
Hydrogen just wants two.

00:08:42.371 --> 00:08:44.174
So we called it out
here, in the recipe.

00:08:44.174 --> 00:08:46.209
Two for hydrogen. OK.

00:08:46.209 --> 00:08:49.112
So add enough electrons
to form an octet.

00:08:49.112 --> 00:08:53.083
Well see, each H
atom already has--

00:08:53.083 --> 00:08:54.451
Remember they're sharing.

00:08:54.451 --> 00:08:55.352
They're sharing.

00:08:55.352 --> 00:08:55.952
OK?

00:08:55.952 --> 00:09:01.691
So each H atom already has the
two electrons that it wants.

00:09:01.691 --> 00:09:05.095
And so, for four I've got that--

00:09:05.095 --> 00:09:07.163
oh, I started with the terminal.

00:09:07.163 --> 00:09:07.664
Atoms.

00:09:07.664 --> 00:09:08.365
OK.

00:09:08.365 --> 00:09:16.940
And so for four, I've
got each H is OK.

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:18.174
It has two.

00:09:18.174 --> 00:09:18.875
All right?

00:09:18.875 --> 00:09:24.848
It has two electrons, which is
what it wants in its valence.

00:09:24.848 --> 00:09:25.348
Next one.

00:09:25.348 --> 00:09:26.116
If-- oh!

00:09:26.116 --> 00:09:29.786
Now, it gets-- If there's
electrons left over

00:09:29.786 --> 00:09:32.088
put them on the central atom.

00:09:32.088 --> 00:09:32.589
OK.

00:09:32.589 --> 00:09:34.224
So I went from the terminal in.

00:09:34.224 --> 00:09:35.592
I went from the outside in.

00:09:35.592 --> 00:09:36.626
H has two.

00:09:36.626 --> 00:09:37.193
H has two.

00:09:37.193 --> 00:09:38.862
That's good.

00:09:38.862 --> 00:09:39.362
Oh!

00:09:39.362 --> 00:09:41.131
But how do I know if
I have any left over?

00:09:41.131 --> 00:09:43.400
Well, I did that in step two.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:45.669
I had a total
number of electrons.

00:09:45.669 --> 00:09:47.404
Now, how many have I used?

00:09:47.404 --> 00:09:50.974
I've used two here,
and I've used two here.

00:09:50.974 --> 00:09:54.277
I have four left over,
because I started with eight.

00:09:54.277 --> 00:09:54.778
Right?

00:09:54.778 --> 00:09:57.180
And I was just
told from step five

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:58.682
to put those on
the central atom.

00:09:58.682 --> 00:09:59.182
OK.

00:09:59.182 --> 00:10:03.253
So step five now, is going to
make this look interesting.

00:10:03.253 --> 00:10:06.456
So H, O, H--

00:10:06.456 --> 00:10:10.360
and now, I'm not going
to put them in a bond.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:10.860
Right?

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:12.262
I'm just going to
put them on the atom.

00:10:12.262 --> 00:10:13.797
And those are
called non-bonding.

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:18.134
Well, OK.

00:10:18.134 --> 00:10:19.302
That's a genius name.

00:10:19.302 --> 00:10:20.003
Nonbinding.

00:10:20.003 --> 00:10:22.138
Because they're not bonding.

00:10:22.138 --> 00:10:23.039
[LAUGHS]

00:10:23.039 --> 00:10:26.810
That's a good name
for those electrons.

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:30.847
You can also call those lone
pairs, because they're lone.

00:10:30.847 --> 00:10:32.282
They're not involved in a bond.

00:10:32.282 --> 00:10:32.782
All right?

00:10:32.782 --> 00:10:35.218
So those are my four
extra electrons.

00:10:35.218 --> 00:10:41.324
And then finally, if the central
atom is less than an octet,

00:10:41.324 --> 00:10:43.026
used the lone pairs
from terminal atoms--

00:10:43.026 --> 00:10:44.260
Oh, this looks complicated.

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:45.362
I don't want to deal with it.

00:10:45.362 --> 00:10:45.862
Why?

00:10:45.862 --> 00:10:50.400
Because I've got an
octet here already.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:54.070
So six is not needed.

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:59.376
Because everything
since the oxygen--

00:10:59.376 --> 00:11:04.214
since O is happy.

00:11:04.214 --> 00:11:06.216
It's got its octet.

00:11:06.216 --> 00:11:10.353
So that is a very slow--

00:11:10.353 --> 00:11:11.187
we went slow.

00:11:11.187 --> 00:11:12.522
We'll go faster now.

00:11:12.522 --> 00:11:13.023
Right?

00:11:13.023 --> 00:11:14.624
So that is a very
slow introduction

00:11:14.624 --> 00:11:18.428
to how to do a Lewis dot
diagram for a molecule.

00:11:18.428 --> 00:11:19.896
In this case, H2O.

00:11:19.896 --> 00:11:24.067
Now, you may notice
I drew it linear.

00:11:24.067 --> 00:11:27.771
Some of us know that is not
the shape of this molecule.

00:11:27.771 --> 00:11:29.005
But shapes come later.

00:11:29.005 --> 00:11:31.207
Shapes come next week, right?

00:11:31.207 --> 00:11:33.343
Right now we're not
thinking about shapes.

00:11:33.343 --> 00:11:34.377
Let's do another example.

00:11:34.377 --> 00:11:42.719
So my next example is O, Cl.

00:11:42.719 --> 00:11:45.588
I'm taking this and I'm
putting a charge on it.

00:11:45.588 --> 00:11:48.858
So here's my next example.

00:11:48.858 --> 00:11:51.161
Well, anyway, that's my example.

00:11:51.161 --> 00:11:52.996
I'm going to go through
the numbers again.

00:11:52.996 --> 00:11:54.431
We're going to go
a little faster.

00:11:54.431 --> 00:11:57.300
So I want to know how
this looks in a Lewis.

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:00.136
Structure well, OK.

00:12:00.136 --> 00:12:03.173
So there's only two atoms, so--

00:12:03.173 --> 00:12:06.776
Maybe I should have called
this example B, because now I

00:12:06.776 --> 00:12:08.178
have point 1, all right?

00:12:08.178 --> 00:12:09.279
So there's only two atoms.

00:12:09.279 --> 00:12:10.747
So there's no central atom.

00:12:10.747 --> 00:12:14.150
OK, that sounds like
that might be easy.

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:17.554
And then step two is, how many
valence electrons are there?

00:12:17.554 --> 00:12:21.124
Well, there's six, plus
seven, plus one, right?

00:12:21.124 --> 00:12:23.460
So oxygen is six.

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:30.133
Chlorine is seven, plus
one, equals 14 electrons.

00:12:30.133 --> 00:12:31.334
Where did the one come from?

00:12:31.334 --> 00:12:33.937
That's because it's got
a negative charge here.

00:12:33.937 --> 00:12:35.605
It's a negatively
charged molecule.

00:12:35.605 --> 00:12:36.106
OK.

00:12:36.106 --> 00:12:36.606
Good.

00:12:36.606 --> 00:12:38.441
Now-- OK, now it
gets interesting.

00:12:38.441 --> 00:12:41.511
Place bonding pair of electrons
between adjacent atoms.

00:12:41.511 --> 00:12:44.347
So my adjacent atoms
are these two atoms,

00:12:44.347 --> 00:12:49.652
which I could write like
this, or I could also

00:12:49.652 --> 00:12:52.255
have written it like this.

00:12:52.255 --> 00:12:54.790
OK?

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:56.960
Those are equivalent.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:57.894
Now step four.

00:12:57.894 --> 00:12:58.394
OK.

00:12:58.394 --> 00:13:00.063
Now, starting with
the terminal atoms,

00:13:00.063 --> 00:13:02.599
add enough electrons to
each one to form an octet.

00:13:02.599 --> 00:13:04.067
So let's go through this.

00:13:04.067 --> 00:13:06.503
So now step four.

00:13:06.503 --> 00:13:07.003
OK.

00:13:11.141 --> 00:13:16.513
Now, this can count
the two electrons.

00:13:16.513 --> 00:13:17.981
This can count
those two electrons.

00:13:17.981 --> 00:13:18.948
You see how this works?

00:13:18.948 --> 00:13:20.984
Because we're sharing now.

00:13:20.984 --> 00:13:22.252
No one took.

00:13:22.252 --> 00:13:22.852
We're sharing.

00:13:22.852 --> 00:13:25.555
So they can each
count those, because

00:13:25.555 --> 00:13:28.358
of what I drew up there in terms
of how a convenient bond works.

00:13:28.358 --> 00:13:32.428
But now, they need another
six to form an octet.

00:13:32.428 --> 00:13:33.563
So how many?

00:13:33.563 --> 00:13:34.964
I used two.

00:13:34.964 --> 00:13:35.965
How many do I have left?

00:13:35.965 --> 00:13:37.867
I've got 12 left.

00:13:37.867 --> 00:13:39.102
It's perfect.

00:13:39.102 --> 00:13:39.602
Right?

00:13:39.602 --> 00:13:40.470
It's perfect.

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:42.772
So I've got exactly
as many as I want.

00:13:42.772 --> 00:13:46.776
And I can write O
has all of those.

00:13:46.776 --> 00:13:49.212
Cl has all of these.

00:13:49.212 --> 00:13:51.414
And if we want to
be careful about it,

00:13:51.414 --> 00:13:54.951
we will emphasize that this
molecule is not neutral.

00:13:54.951 --> 00:13:56.419
It got an extra electron.

00:13:56.419 --> 00:13:58.688
That's why we put the
minus sign up there.

00:13:58.688 --> 00:13:59.189
Right?

00:13:59.189 --> 00:14:01.424
It's charged.

00:14:01.424 --> 00:14:03.760
And this molecule, OK.

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:05.328
Each atom-- let's see, OK.

00:14:05.328 --> 00:14:07.030
I used the extra electrons.

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:08.331
If electrons are left over--

00:14:08.331 --> 00:14:10.700
No electrons left over.

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:15.038
If central atoms-- no,
they're already octets.

00:14:15.038 --> 00:14:19.108
So five and six are not needed.

00:14:19.108 --> 00:14:21.978
Oh, but you know
the last example

00:14:21.978 --> 00:14:23.913
is going to be more interesting.

00:14:23.913 --> 00:14:26.249
So let's do that.

00:14:26.249 --> 00:14:27.784
Last example.

00:14:27.784 --> 00:14:31.754
O-- C, example C. O--

00:14:31.754 --> 00:14:35.191
so I've got H, OK.

00:14:35.191 --> 00:14:36.793
I'm just going to
write the atoms here,

00:14:36.793 --> 00:14:39.963
because I'm starting
where I don't even know

00:14:39.963 --> 00:14:41.164
what it's going to look like.

00:14:41.164 --> 00:14:42.732
I don't even want to hint at it.

00:14:42.732 --> 00:14:43.499
Those are my atoms.

00:14:43.499 --> 00:14:47.503
I've got two hydrogens,
an oxygen, and a chlorine.

00:14:47.503 --> 00:14:48.004
OK.

00:14:48.004 --> 00:14:49.339
I'm sorry, a carbon.

00:14:49.339 --> 00:14:50.406
Oh, yeah.

00:14:50.406 --> 00:14:52.842
I meant this to be a carbon.

00:14:52.842 --> 00:14:55.211
There we go.

00:14:55.211 --> 00:14:57.614
Two hydrogens, an
oxygen, and carbon.

00:14:57.614 --> 00:14:58.147
OK.

00:14:58.147 --> 00:15:01.851
Now, as we will see when I tell
you what electronegativity is,

00:15:01.851 --> 00:15:04.387
carbon has the lowest
electronegativity.

00:15:04.387 --> 00:15:05.154
OK?

00:15:05.154 --> 00:15:09.425
And so, I'm going to
put that in the center.

00:15:09.425 --> 00:15:14.364
That's going to tell me
that if I answer point 1,

00:15:14.364 --> 00:15:16.900
it might look
something like this.

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:20.169
Oh, now it's taking
some form, right?

00:15:20.169 --> 00:15:20.670
OK?

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:22.472
So the hydrogens are
going to go like that.

00:15:22.472 --> 00:15:26.976
Their terminally oxygen is
outside, carbons in the middle.

00:15:26.976 --> 00:15:28.378
And how many
electrons do I have?

00:15:28.378 --> 00:15:29.846
OK, so now, let's see.

00:15:29.846 --> 00:15:37.654
Answering step two, I've
got 12 valence electrons.

00:15:37.654 --> 00:15:38.521
OK.

00:15:38.521 --> 00:15:43.459
Because I've got six,
four, five, right?

00:15:43.459 --> 00:15:44.794
So 12.

00:15:44.794 --> 00:15:45.295
OK?

00:15:45.295 --> 00:15:46.863
Good.

00:15:46.863 --> 00:15:49.032
And now, I'm going to put
the bonding pair in there.

00:15:49.032 --> 00:15:50.500
So let's do that.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:52.035
So step three--

00:15:52.035 --> 00:15:59.242
OK, so now I've got C, H, H,
O. So I've done step three.

00:15:59.242 --> 00:15:59.742
OK.

00:15:59.742 --> 00:16:01.544
So I've got two, four, six.

00:16:01.544 --> 00:16:07.850
I've used up six electrons
in this sharing covalent bond

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:11.454
situation, and I
started with 12.

00:16:11.454 --> 00:16:14.657
So that means I've got six left.

00:16:14.657 --> 00:16:15.158
OK.

00:16:15.158 --> 00:16:16.659
So I'm going to go to step four.

00:16:16.659 --> 00:16:19.195
Now, starting with the terminal
atoms, add enough to each one

00:16:19.195 --> 00:16:20.029
to form an octet.

00:16:20.029 --> 00:16:21.965
Now hydrogen is good, right?

00:16:21.965 --> 00:16:24.267
Because hydrogen, remember,
it's octet is a doublet.

00:16:24.267 --> 00:16:25.301
It's two.

00:16:25.301 --> 00:16:25.902
So that's good.

00:16:25.902 --> 00:16:27.136
But what about oxygen?

00:16:27.136 --> 00:16:27.637
OK.

00:16:27.637 --> 00:16:29.772
It looks like oxygen needs some.

00:16:29.772 --> 00:16:33.609
So now, we're going to write
this structure like this.

00:16:33.609 --> 00:16:38.982
I've got to add six more
electrons to oxygen in order

00:16:38.982 --> 00:16:40.450
to fulfill its octet, right?

00:16:40.450 --> 00:16:46.656
And oxygen is happy.

00:16:46.656 --> 00:16:47.156
All right?

00:16:47.156 --> 00:16:50.293
Yeah, it is.

00:16:50.293 --> 00:16:51.861
Just like that.

00:16:51.861 --> 00:16:54.397
But if electrons are
left over-- but are

00:16:54.397 --> 00:16:57.467
there any electrons left over?

00:16:57.467 --> 00:16:58.601
No.

00:16:58.601 --> 00:17:00.503
So I can't place electrons
on the center atom,

00:17:00.503 --> 00:17:04.173
yet now we invoke number six.

00:17:04.173 --> 00:17:06.041
If central less than an octet--

00:17:06.041 --> 00:17:10.012
it is two, two, two--

00:17:10.012 --> 00:17:12.214
then use lone pairs
from terminal atoms

00:17:12.214 --> 00:17:14.617
to form multiple bonds
to the central atom.

00:17:14.617 --> 00:17:15.885
Oh, yeah.

00:17:15.885 --> 00:17:17.319
Now we're there.

00:17:17.319 --> 00:17:17.987
Now we're there.

00:17:17.987 --> 00:17:19.589
So now we jump to step six.

00:17:22.759 --> 00:17:23.760
Because look.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:24.560
Let's continue.

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:28.031
Because look, if I add all
these up and I go to carbon,

00:17:28.031 --> 00:17:28.931
it's not happy.

00:17:31.601 --> 00:17:35.405
But let's make it happy, because
that's what Lewis lets us do.

00:17:35.405 --> 00:17:39.008
And I'm going to
go down to here--

00:17:39.008 --> 00:17:40.910
six continued.

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:44.680
And all I need to
do to do that is

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:49.752
to follow the instructions,
which is, taking one lone pair

00:17:49.752 --> 00:17:51.287
and put it here.

00:17:51.287 --> 00:17:55.124
So I'm going to draw these
explicitly now as dots, right?

00:17:55.124 --> 00:17:58.127
So I took one of those lone
pairs and I moved it here.

00:17:58.127 --> 00:18:01.197
And I created a
stronger sharing bond.

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:08.404
I wrote down the guidelines
of Lewis, remember, on Monday.

00:18:08.404 --> 00:18:10.840
And the third point was octet!

00:18:10.840 --> 00:18:11.340
Right?

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:13.609
Atoms want to achieve
their nearest octet.

00:18:13.609 --> 00:18:17.013
And this this tells
us then, by following

00:18:17.013 --> 00:18:22.151
these steps to implement
Lewis' dream and vision,

00:18:22.151 --> 00:18:25.788
we get nothing less than
the nature of bonding itself

00:18:25.788 --> 00:18:27.156
in molecules.

00:18:27.156 --> 00:18:28.858
That's cool.

00:18:28.858 --> 00:18:32.662
And so you could write this
with sticks if you want.

00:18:32.662 --> 00:18:37.867
C, H-- that's a double bond
with those electrons up there.

00:18:37.867 --> 00:18:39.202
It's the same thing.

00:18:39.202 --> 00:18:41.938
And now, if you count--

00:18:41.938 --> 00:18:45.041
happy, happy, happy, happy.

00:18:45.041 --> 00:18:47.443
Everybody's happy.

00:18:47.443 --> 00:18:49.378
Caring is sharing.

00:18:49.378 --> 00:18:50.213
They're all sharing.

00:18:52.815 --> 00:18:56.853
So this is the
general idea of Lewis.

00:18:56.853 --> 00:19:00.323
Following the Lewis prescription
tells us about covalent bonds,

00:19:00.323 --> 00:19:02.358
and it tells us
how many electrons

00:19:02.358 --> 00:19:03.326
are going to be shared.

00:19:03.326 --> 00:19:06.162
Now, it also tells us something
else that's very important,

00:19:06.162 --> 00:19:08.397
and it's through this
concept called formal charge.

00:19:08.397 --> 00:19:12.602
So that's the next thing
that we need to learn.

00:19:12.602 --> 00:19:14.570
Because now, I go
to another molecule.

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:16.506
In this case, it's formaldehyde.

00:19:16.506 --> 00:19:17.039
OK.

00:19:17.039 --> 00:19:18.641
CH2O.

00:19:18.641 --> 00:19:21.911
But look, now, neither
of these is done.

00:19:21.911 --> 00:19:23.012
These are not done.

00:19:23.012 --> 00:19:25.248
But it's like my starting place.

00:19:25.248 --> 00:19:25.748
OK.

00:19:25.748 --> 00:19:26.816
Carbon central here.

00:19:26.816 --> 00:19:29.152
It's kind of central-ish there.

00:19:29.152 --> 00:19:32.421
Can I start with
either of these?

00:19:32.421 --> 00:19:34.757
And the answer is yes, you can.

00:19:34.757 --> 00:19:36.259
And you can draw--

00:19:36.259 --> 00:19:38.895
and let's do that--
you can actually

00:19:38.895 --> 00:19:42.231
draw correct Lewis structures.

00:19:42.231 --> 00:19:43.533
Let me use the board over here.

00:19:50.206 --> 00:19:51.207
All right?

00:19:51.207 --> 00:19:54.410
So I could draw this like this.

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:57.980
H-- Let me see here.

00:19:57.980 --> 00:20:06.255
H, C, double bond, O, hydrogen.
And I could put that there

00:20:06.255 --> 00:20:07.690
and that there.

00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:10.326
Or I could draw it like this.

00:20:10.326 --> 00:20:11.460
Do I have it this way?

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:12.228
Yes.

00:20:12.228 --> 00:20:12.795
All right?

00:20:12.795 --> 00:20:13.863
Like this.

00:20:13.863 --> 00:20:17.300
And carbon double bond O.

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:22.572
And when I look at this, and
I look at the electron count,

00:20:22.572 --> 00:20:23.806
it all works out.

00:20:23.806 --> 00:20:25.841
All right?

00:20:25.841 --> 00:20:28.110
I got to add my lone
pairs to the oxygen.

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:30.446
Don't let me forget those.

00:20:30.446 --> 00:20:32.415
It all works out.

00:20:32.415 --> 00:20:36.052
The total number of valence
electrons is 12 in both cases,

00:20:36.052 --> 00:20:40.656
and everybody's
obeying the octet rule.

00:20:40.656 --> 00:20:43.259
But the concept of
formal charge allows

00:20:43.259 --> 00:20:45.962
us to distinguish
between which one

00:20:45.962 --> 00:20:48.564
is likely to be more stable.

00:20:48.564 --> 00:20:51.601
And so, what formal charge is--
and I've got it written out

00:20:51.601 --> 00:20:53.069
here--

00:20:53.069 --> 00:20:54.203
OK?

00:20:54.203 --> 00:20:56.339
So formal charge--
non-binding electrons

00:20:56.339 --> 00:20:57.473
count for the given atom.

00:20:57.473 --> 00:20:59.775
Bonding electrons
are divided equally.

00:20:59.775 --> 00:21:04.013
And so formal charge is the
total valence of an atom--

00:21:04.013 --> 00:21:07.149
so I can take atom here and
say, what's it's total valence?

00:21:07.149 --> 00:21:08.017
Carbon, four.

00:21:08.017 --> 00:21:09.485
Oxygen, six.

00:21:09.485 --> 00:21:11.554
All right?

00:21:11.554 --> 00:21:13.923
So that's the total
balance of the free atom.

00:21:13.923 --> 00:21:16.459
And then I subtract
from that how many

00:21:16.459 --> 00:21:22.265
nonbinding electrons it has,
plus the bonding ones over two.

00:21:22.265 --> 00:21:22.765
Right?

00:21:22.765 --> 00:21:25.067
And so, if I do that--

00:21:25.067 --> 00:21:26.936
let's write that.

00:21:26.936 --> 00:21:29.739
So if I think about that--

00:21:29.739 --> 00:21:44.920
gesundheit-- from my
pictures, the formal charge

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:54.263
is equal to the number
of valence electrons

00:21:54.263 --> 00:21:55.231
of the free atom.

00:21:55.231 --> 00:21:58.668
That means the
atom, before it ever

00:21:58.668 --> 00:22:05.908
participated in a bond,
minus the number of dots,

00:22:05.908 --> 00:22:08.077
minus the number of lines.

00:22:11.414 --> 00:22:16.485
Because that is really the
same definition, right?

00:22:16.485 --> 00:22:19.722
That's the same definition.

00:22:19.722 --> 00:22:21.991
So let's go up to this one.

00:22:21.991 --> 00:22:28.831
So now, if I look at carbon
in this configuration,

00:22:28.831 --> 00:22:30.833
and I calculate the
formal charge of carbon,

00:22:30.833 --> 00:22:32.902
it's going to be 4--

00:22:32.902 --> 00:22:36.038
the valence electron
of the free atom--

00:22:36.038 --> 00:22:42.411
minus 2-- because it's got
the two dots there, right?

00:22:42.411 --> 00:22:45.114
Minus 3-- 1, 2, 3.

00:22:45.114 --> 00:22:47.750
Or the total number of
electrons involved in bonds

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:48.617
divided by two--

00:22:48.617 --> 00:22:49.652
6 over 2.

00:22:49.652 --> 00:22:52.254
3, right?

00:22:52.254 --> 00:22:53.489
So those are equivalent.

00:22:53.489 --> 00:22:59.061
But that means that the
formal charge on this is--

00:22:59.061 --> 00:23:05.868
just spell it out here for now--
formal charge is equal to -1.

00:23:05.868 --> 00:23:10.339
And if I do the same
thing here, then it's +1.

00:23:10.339 --> 00:23:13.843
But you see, if I look
at this structure,

00:23:13.843 --> 00:23:16.245
and I think about the formal
charge on the carbon, atom--

00:23:16.245 --> 00:23:23.753
Again, I'm basically counting
a change from the free atom.

00:23:23.753 --> 00:23:24.820
OK?

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:26.055
And so carbon had four.

00:23:28.758 --> 00:23:31.327
What did this new
bonding environment

00:23:31.327 --> 00:23:34.930
do to the valence chemistry?

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:36.999
And in this case, the
formal charge on carbon

00:23:36.999 --> 00:23:39.168
is going to be 4--

00:23:39.168 --> 00:23:41.704
starting with the number
of free valence electrons--

00:23:41.704 --> 00:23:48.177
minus no dots, but it's got
one, two, three, four bonds.

00:23:48.177 --> 00:23:49.879
So that's zero.

00:23:49.879 --> 00:23:51.814
And the formal
charge on the oxygen

00:23:51.814 --> 00:23:56.051
is going to be 6 minus
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.

00:23:56.051 --> 00:23:58.020
So that's also zero.

00:23:58.020 --> 00:23:59.455
And you get a
sense-- how much did

00:23:59.455 --> 00:24:05.394
I push this atom away from
where it normally thinks

00:24:05.394 --> 00:24:08.063
about its charge balance?

00:24:08.063 --> 00:24:10.032
And you get a sense for
why this might be true.

00:24:10.032 --> 00:24:13.669
Lewis structure with a set of
formal charges closest to 0

00:24:13.669 --> 00:24:15.471
is usually the most stable.

00:24:15.471 --> 00:24:16.906
And that's our guideline.

00:24:16.906 --> 00:24:22.044
And so, this one not very happy.

00:24:22.044 --> 00:24:25.714
That one happy.

00:24:25.714 --> 00:24:29.218
And so you can calculate the
formal charge on any atom

00:24:29.218 --> 00:24:32.054
in any Lewis
structure-- gesundheit--

00:24:32.054 --> 00:24:35.391
by following this
very simple procedure.

00:24:35.391 --> 00:24:39.795
You take any atom and you look
at how you've drawn it's Lewis

00:24:39.795 --> 00:24:41.464
structure, right?

00:24:41.464 --> 00:24:43.632
And you just count.

00:24:43.632 --> 00:24:47.903
So for example, if I
take CO2, and I think,

00:24:47.903 --> 00:24:49.805
well, I can do the
same thing here.

00:24:49.805 --> 00:24:53.642
I've got two Lewis
structures and they look like

00:24:53.642 --> 00:24:55.911
they both satisfy the recipe.

00:24:55.911 --> 00:24:58.180
The recipe is satisfied.

00:24:58.180 --> 00:25:00.282
But which one is more stable?

00:25:00.282 --> 00:25:04.086
Well, if I look at this,
it's the same idea.

00:25:04.086 --> 00:25:08.190
And I won't go
through it in detail,

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:10.359
but if I look at this then--

00:25:10.359 --> 00:25:17.733
C, O, O, with lone pairs
on the Os, like that.

00:25:17.733 --> 00:25:18.300
All right?

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:25.174
This is going to give me
formal charges of 0, 0, and 0.

00:25:25.174 --> 00:25:29.211
Whereas, if I do the one on the
right and I put a triple bond--

00:25:29.211 --> 00:25:31.947
so I put too many
electrons sharing--

00:25:31.947 --> 00:25:35.651
then yeah, I
satisfied the octets,

00:25:35.651 --> 00:25:39.355
but no, I'm not as stable,
because the formal charges

00:25:39.355 --> 00:25:40.689
aren't as close to zero.

00:25:43.592 --> 00:25:47.897
So this gives me to my brief,
why this matters for take.

00:25:47.897 --> 00:25:52.535
Why would I care which
one of these CO2 takes?

00:25:52.535 --> 00:25:53.135
All right?

00:25:53.135 --> 00:25:53.636
OK.

00:25:53.636 --> 00:25:59.808
Well, it turns out that as long
as we're not at absolute zero,

00:25:59.808 --> 00:26:03.078
this molecule is moving.

00:26:03.078 --> 00:26:08.651
And so, obviously,
that's related to this.

00:26:08.651 --> 00:26:09.552
It's not obvious.

00:26:09.552 --> 00:26:16.458
But cars in the US are now
the number one emitter of CO2.

00:26:16.458 --> 00:26:16.959
OK?

00:26:16.959 --> 00:26:21.096
They're the number one
cause of CO2 emissions.

00:26:21.096 --> 00:26:24.867
And here's a nice
little corner somewhere.

00:26:24.867 --> 00:26:28.804
And so, this is coming out now
from the transportation sector.

00:26:31.941 --> 00:26:35.144
More of the CO2 comes from
that than any other sector.

00:26:35.144 --> 00:26:36.011
Why does it matter?

00:26:36.011 --> 00:26:39.848
Well, why does this
Lewis structure matter?

00:26:39.848 --> 00:26:41.717
Well, it has to do
with how it moves.

00:26:41.717 --> 00:26:46.755
Because, you see, I
showed you this before.

00:26:46.755 --> 00:26:50.225
And I didn't complete it because
we were talking about electron

00:26:50.225 --> 00:26:53.062
transitions, and then
I gave you the example

00:26:53.062 --> 00:26:56.832
of ozone being really important
for absorbing in the UV.

00:26:56.832 --> 00:26:59.902
And I showed you the chemistry
of ozone degradation with CFCs.

00:26:59.902 --> 00:27:02.371
You see, look at this
absorption out here.

00:27:02.371 --> 00:27:04.373
There's CO2, right?

00:27:04.373 --> 00:27:06.442
And so, there's the sunlight
above the atmosphere.

00:27:06.442 --> 00:27:07.810
Here it is on Earth.

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:09.979
And you could see CO2 there.

00:27:09.979 --> 00:27:13.716
But see, the reason
CO2 absorbs there

00:27:13.716 --> 00:27:15.884
has to do with how it moves.

00:27:15.884 --> 00:27:19.088
It has to do with
its vibrations.

00:27:19.088 --> 00:27:20.522
Now, that's not
something that you

00:27:20.522 --> 00:27:23.892
need to know for like
a test or something,

00:27:23.892 --> 00:27:26.328
but I wanted to tell you about
it because it is directly

00:27:26.328 --> 00:27:28.464
related to what we just did.

00:27:28.464 --> 00:27:30.232
Those vibrations--
and by the way,

00:27:30.232 --> 00:27:34.970
that first mode is the one
out here doing all the IR

00:27:34.970 --> 00:27:36.105
absorption.

00:27:36.105 --> 00:27:36.605
Right?

00:27:36.605 --> 00:27:37.673
This one.

00:27:37.673 --> 00:27:41.777
And you can see right away,
if this thing is wiggling,

00:27:41.777 --> 00:27:43.812
it's going to wiggle
very differently.

00:27:43.812 --> 00:27:44.313
Right?

00:27:44.313 --> 00:27:46.682
It's going to wiggle very
differently whether it

00:27:46.682 --> 00:27:49.685
has two double bonds on
either side of the carbon,

00:27:49.685 --> 00:27:51.053
or a triple and a single.

00:27:51.053 --> 00:27:51.553
Right?

00:27:51.553 --> 00:27:53.188
And so that, alone,
tells you something

00:27:53.188 --> 00:27:55.324
really important
about how it interacts

00:27:55.324 --> 00:27:59.595
in our atmosphere with
radiant heat, right?

00:27:59.595 --> 00:28:02.197
With IR radiation.

00:28:02.197 --> 00:28:03.098
OK.

00:28:03.098 --> 00:28:05.100
That's my 'why this matters.'

00:28:05.100 --> 00:28:13.075
Now, I've been talking about
all these things in absolutes.

00:28:13.075 --> 00:28:18.247
But you see, the world
is not so absolute.

00:28:18.247 --> 00:28:22.217
And in fact, we're missing
a really important part

00:28:22.217 --> 00:28:24.019
of the picture here.

00:28:24.019 --> 00:28:27.322
So this is H2, right?

00:28:27.322 --> 00:28:31.427
There is an iconic bond where,
remember, it's like, I'm picky!

00:28:31.427 --> 00:28:31.994
OK.

00:28:31.994 --> 00:28:34.029
And then it's like a
Coulomb interaction.

00:28:34.029 --> 00:28:35.798
And here it's a sharing.

00:28:35.798 --> 00:28:38.701
Each electron sees
both of those protons.

00:28:38.701 --> 00:28:42.805
But there's a whole bunch
of room in between, right?

00:28:42.805 --> 00:28:44.506
There's a whole bunch
of room in between.

00:28:44.506 --> 00:28:49.411
And so what we need next, as
we talk about covalent bonds,

00:28:49.411 --> 00:28:53.515
is we need a way to think
about how ionic they are.

00:28:53.515 --> 00:28:54.349
Right?

00:28:54.349 --> 00:28:57.252
Because there's only a
certain kind of covalent bond

00:28:57.252 --> 00:29:00.856
that's purely covalent.

00:29:00.856 --> 00:29:01.990
As it's written here--

00:29:01.990 --> 00:29:03.926
non-polar covalent.

00:29:03.926 --> 00:29:07.229
So let's talk about that next.

00:29:07.229 --> 00:29:09.164
And what we're going
to do is use this term

00:29:09.164 --> 00:29:10.899
that I alluded to in
the beginning, which

00:29:10.899 --> 00:29:15.003
is point number
one in your recipe,

00:29:15.003 --> 00:29:18.574
and that is electronegativity.

00:29:18.574 --> 00:29:19.074
OK?

00:29:19.074 --> 00:29:21.543
So I'm going to get it done.

00:29:25.514 --> 00:29:27.483
Almost.

00:29:27.483 --> 00:29:29.084
Practice makes perfect.

00:29:29.084 --> 00:29:29.585
OK.

00:29:32.788 --> 00:29:33.555
All right.

00:29:33.555 --> 00:29:38.527
So there is a
symbol kai, that we

00:29:38.527 --> 00:29:44.767
use for this concept
of electronegativity.

00:29:44.767 --> 00:29:48.170
Electronegativity.

00:29:48.170 --> 00:29:49.605
OK?

00:29:49.605 --> 00:30:02.518
Now, this is the
tendency of an atom

00:30:02.518 --> 00:30:19.067
to attract shared pair
of electrons to itself.

00:30:19.067 --> 00:30:19.568
OK?

00:30:23.672 --> 00:30:26.508
Let's write "in a
bond," just to be sure.

00:30:26.508 --> 00:30:27.176
All right?

00:30:27.176 --> 00:30:27.676
In a bond.

00:30:27.676 --> 00:30:33.649
So if an electron in a
bond, the question is,

00:30:33.649 --> 00:30:38.053
how much was it able to pull
those bonding electrons to it?

00:30:38.053 --> 00:30:38.987
All right?

00:30:38.987 --> 00:30:46.461
And you can already think
about this in terms of concepts

00:30:46.461 --> 00:30:48.063
that we've learned already.

00:30:48.063 --> 00:30:48.564
Right?

00:30:48.564 --> 00:30:50.432
Like the size of an atom.

00:30:50.432 --> 00:30:52.067
How far out electrons are?

00:30:52.067 --> 00:30:52.568
Right?

00:30:52.568 --> 00:30:53.735
The radius of an atom.

00:30:53.735 --> 00:30:56.205
Whether there's
shielding going on.

00:30:56.205 --> 00:30:56.839
Right?

00:30:56.839 --> 00:31:00.175
This kind of leads you--
how many protons there are.

00:31:00.175 --> 00:31:00.776
All right?

00:31:00.776 --> 00:31:03.345
This leads you already to
be able to think about this.

00:31:03.345 --> 00:31:07.983
Which atom is going to want a
pair of electrons in a bond,

00:31:07.983 --> 00:31:08.483
right?

00:31:08.483 --> 00:31:10.686
Which one is going to want
it more, and how much?

00:31:10.686 --> 00:31:13.822
But it was Pauling who said,
no, I want to go further that.

00:31:13.822 --> 00:31:15.858
I want to write down--

00:31:15.858 --> 00:31:16.925
even if it's empirical--

00:31:16.925 --> 00:31:19.728
I want to write down
some way, some number.

00:31:19.728 --> 00:31:22.164
I want to quantify this.

00:31:22.164 --> 00:31:26.468
And so he came up with a
scale for electronegativity.

00:31:26.468 --> 00:31:28.403
The term and concept
of electronegativity

00:31:28.403 --> 00:31:32.574
goes back long before Pauling,
to, I think, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:31:32.574 --> 00:31:35.577
But Pauling is the one who
said, I want to quantify this.

00:31:35.577 --> 00:31:37.145
And the way we're
going to do is we're

00:31:37.145 --> 00:31:40.515
going to measure bonding
energies between all sorts

00:31:40.515 --> 00:31:42.351
of different atoms.

00:31:42.351 --> 00:31:45.287
And what he found is
that, if a bonds to a,

00:31:45.287 --> 00:31:49.725
and b bonds to b, a to b is not
just a simple sum of the two.

00:31:49.725 --> 00:31:52.327
And that clued him
in for how to think

00:31:52.327 --> 00:31:57.532
about this partially covalent
or polar covalent nature.

00:31:57.532 --> 00:31:59.368
I'll tell you why that
word "polar" is there

00:31:59.368 --> 00:32:01.303
in a few minutes.

00:32:01.303 --> 00:32:03.538
And so Pauling
developed this scale--

00:32:03.538 --> 00:32:06.208
the electronegativity
scale-- to tell us this.

00:32:06.208 --> 00:32:10.879
And he arbitrarily set
fluorine to four and hydrogen

00:32:10.879 --> 00:32:15.350
at first 2.1, now it's 2.2.

00:32:15.350 --> 00:32:17.586
But basically, this is
just some of the elements

00:32:17.586 --> 00:32:20.255
of the periodic table, and these
are their electronegativities.

00:32:20.255 --> 00:32:21.256
OK?

00:32:21.256 --> 00:32:24.927
And you don't need to worry
about how this scale was

00:32:24.927 --> 00:32:26.094
developed quantitatively.

00:32:26.094 --> 00:32:29.197
There are actually many
electronegativity scales.

00:32:29.197 --> 00:32:29.898
OK?

00:32:29.898 --> 00:32:34.169
What I want you to know is
what it means conceptually,

00:32:34.169 --> 00:32:36.972
and then how to use
it to think about

00:32:36.972 --> 00:32:41.276
whether a bond is going
to be ionic, or covalent,

00:32:41.276 --> 00:32:43.312
or somewhere in between.

00:32:43.312 --> 00:32:45.847
And so, you can see already
here, OK, the extremes of this

00:32:45.847 --> 00:32:47.416
are fluorine and caesium.

00:32:47.416 --> 00:32:50.852
These are the most
electronegative,

00:32:50.852 --> 00:32:55.524
least electronegative, or if you
want to be positive about it,

00:32:55.524 --> 00:32:56.825
most electropositive.

00:32:56.825 --> 00:32:58.527
When you're talking
to these elements

00:32:58.527 --> 00:33:01.296
you want them to feel good.

00:33:01.296 --> 00:33:02.264
They're not the least.

00:33:02.264 --> 00:33:03.598
They're very important.

00:33:03.598 --> 00:33:05.233
So they're electropositive.

00:33:05.233 --> 00:33:06.201
Right?

00:33:06.201 --> 00:33:07.569
And now you know conceptually.

00:33:07.569 --> 00:33:11.773
But look, now we also can
think about differences.

00:33:11.773 --> 00:33:16.411
Because, if the difference
between this concept,

00:33:16.411 --> 00:33:20.849
from one atom to
another is zero,

00:33:20.849 --> 00:33:24.319
well then, that must be
a pure covalent bond.

00:33:24.319 --> 00:33:29.891
Because neither one could
bring in the electron pair

00:33:29.891 --> 00:33:32.728
more than the other, right?

00:33:32.728 --> 00:33:35.163
So like chlorine two--

00:33:35.163 --> 00:33:39.901
the chlorine dimer-- then the
change in electronegativity

00:33:39.901 --> 00:33:45.340
between one chlorine and another
is zero, it's a pure covalent.

00:33:45.340 --> 00:33:47.409
I mean, just think about
that conceptually, right?

00:33:47.409 --> 00:33:52.748
So that's what
electronegativity means.

00:33:52.748 --> 00:33:56.952
So if I have two that are the
same, neither one can draw in.

00:33:56.952 --> 00:33:58.520
They have the same kai.

00:33:58.520 --> 00:34:01.356
Neither one can draw the
pair towards them more.

00:34:01.356 --> 00:34:01.857
Right?

00:34:01.857 --> 00:34:04.693
So the same is true for
any dimer like this,

00:34:04.693 --> 00:34:06.128
where it's the same atom.

00:34:06.128 --> 00:34:10.264
The H2 dimer that we started
with, also has delta kai of 0.

00:34:10.264 --> 00:34:14.436
Those are called pure covalent,
because it's just pure sharing.

00:34:14.436 --> 00:34:16.071
Nobody took more
charge than the other.

00:34:16.071 --> 00:34:19.708
But see, if I go to something
like sodium chloride,

00:34:19.708 --> 00:34:26.081
well now, from this
table I can calculate

00:34:26.081 --> 00:34:27.516
the difference in
electronegativity

00:34:27.516 --> 00:34:29.384
between sodium and chlorine.

00:34:29.384 --> 00:34:37.259
And it's 2.23, which
is pretty high.

00:34:37.259 --> 00:34:40.996
And it's ionic.

00:34:40.996 --> 00:34:45.434
So if that difference is high,
then it means one of the atoms

00:34:45.434 --> 00:34:48.670
grabbed the electrons.

00:34:48.670 --> 00:34:52.841
And if it's kind of
0, it's pure covalent.

00:34:52.841 --> 00:34:54.476
And if it's
somewhere in between,

00:34:54.476 --> 00:34:55.911
it's this polar covalent.

00:34:55.911 --> 00:34:58.013
So, for example, HCL.

00:34:58.013 --> 00:35:00.615
So these are all with
the same atom-- chlorine.

00:35:00.615 --> 00:35:07.255
But here delta kai
is equal to 0.96.

00:35:07.255 --> 00:35:11.393
So this is called
polar covalent.

00:35:15.130 --> 00:35:17.165
OK?

00:35:17.165 --> 00:35:21.069
So this is also
partially ionic, right?

00:35:21.069 --> 00:35:22.704
These mean the same things.

00:35:22.704 --> 00:35:27.008
There's ionic character-- is
another way that we say it.

00:35:27.008 --> 00:35:31.613
This bond is not purely
covalent because one of them

00:35:31.613 --> 00:35:34.716
took a little more charge,
because it's electronegativity

00:35:34.716 --> 00:35:36.118
was higher.

00:35:36.118 --> 00:35:38.086
And therefore,
there's a little bit

00:35:38.086 --> 00:35:40.021
of that kind of
ionic thing going on,

00:35:40.021 --> 00:35:43.091
because you got a little plus
over here, and a little minus

00:35:43.091 --> 00:35:44.392
over there.

00:35:44.392 --> 00:35:46.161
Right?

00:35:46.161 --> 00:35:59.007
And that is
something that can be

00:35:59.007 --> 00:36:03.011
looked at for a whole
bunch of different bonds.

00:36:03.011 --> 00:36:06.314
And so here, this is what
Pauling was trying to fit.

00:36:06.314 --> 00:36:08.984
So he came up with
these empirical fits.

00:36:08.984 --> 00:36:10.018
Right?

00:36:10.018 --> 00:36:15.624
OK, things kind of tend
to lie on this line, which

00:36:15.624 --> 00:36:18.226
is the plot of the
electronegativity difference

00:36:18.226 --> 00:36:24.032
between two different atoms
the percent ionic character.

00:36:24.032 --> 00:36:25.667
Is it really fully ionic?

00:36:25.667 --> 00:36:27.002
Is it is it fully covalent?

00:36:27.002 --> 00:36:30.205
And you can see, over here, it's
kind of not interesting, right?

00:36:30.205 --> 00:36:32.707
It's just anything
with itself is zero.

00:36:32.707 --> 00:36:36.311
And over here, you've got
some of the same ionic bonds

00:36:36.311 --> 00:36:38.547
that we've already
talked about, right?

00:36:38.547 --> 00:36:40.849
And then you've got all
this stuff in between.

00:36:40.849 --> 00:36:44.252
And what this says is, as
you've now seen, right?

00:36:44.252 --> 00:36:47.389
[INAUDIBLE] is a
suggestion of a rule,

00:36:47.389 --> 00:36:50.158
and then it gets
broken 20% of the time.

00:36:50.158 --> 00:36:53.828
This is also a way of
qualitatively classifying

00:36:53.828 --> 00:36:57.832
bonds and thinking about the
ionic character in a bond,

00:36:57.832 --> 00:37:00.802
but it is not
always quantitative.

00:37:00.802 --> 00:37:03.672
But in general,
what you'll see is

00:37:03.672 --> 00:37:09.010
that if delta kai is
greater than 2, then

00:37:09.010 --> 00:37:11.613
it tends to be an ionic bond.

00:37:11.613 --> 00:37:17.752
And if delta kai is less
than, let's say, 1.6,

00:37:17.752 --> 00:37:22.257
it tends to be covalent
or polar covalent.

00:37:27.429 --> 00:37:31.066
And in-between it depends.

00:37:31.066 --> 00:37:32.000
How do I know?

00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:32.500
Right?

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:35.203
I'll show you an example.

00:37:35.203 --> 00:37:36.037
Where's my example?

00:37:36.037 --> 00:37:37.772
I've got an example here.

00:37:37.772 --> 00:37:38.707
Sodium bromide.

00:37:38.707 --> 00:37:39.841
It's not on there.

00:37:39.841 --> 00:37:47.582
But if I take sodium
bromide and I take HF,

00:37:47.582 --> 00:37:53.822
they both have
delta kai equal 1.9.

00:37:53.822 --> 00:37:57.359
Now, some textbooks
will simply say 1.7.

00:37:57.359 --> 00:37:59.594
You will see that
in some textbooks,

00:37:59.594 --> 00:38:01.863
because they just want
it to be all or nothing.

00:38:01.863 --> 00:38:03.131
It's always ionic, or it's--

00:38:03.131 --> 00:38:04.532
But the fact of
the matter is that

00:38:04.532 --> 00:38:08.837
in this intermediate
regime you get variations

00:38:08.837 --> 00:38:10.472
that depend on other things.

00:38:10.472 --> 00:38:15.810
And so, for example, HF is
a gas at room temperature.

00:38:15.810 --> 00:38:17.979
So these have the same
electronegativity difference,

00:38:17.979 --> 00:38:22.584
but this really behaves like
a polar covalent molecule.

00:38:22.584 --> 00:38:25.820
And this really behaves
like an ionic solid--

00:38:25.820 --> 00:38:26.755
an ionic bond.

00:38:26.755 --> 00:38:28.056
How do you know?

00:38:28.056 --> 00:38:29.658
It's the properties.

00:38:29.658 --> 00:38:30.191
Right?

00:38:30.191 --> 00:38:32.360
Remember, I showed
you the properties

00:38:32.360 --> 00:38:35.997
of ionic solids on Monday.

00:38:35.997 --> 00:38:37.999
And so, I can look at
those and check them off--

00:38:37.999 --> 00:38:39.801
solid at room
temperature, et cetera.

00:38:39.801 --> 00:38:41.870
And I can see, well,
does this fit the bill?

00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:43.471
Does it look like
an iconic solid?

00:38:43.471 --> 00:38:45.273
It absolutely does not.

00:38:45.273 --> 00:38:47.542
So even though this
is the same, you

00:38:47.542 --> 00:38:50.945
got to be careful in
this intermediate regime.

00:38:50.945 --> 00:38:53.715
Now, you can also
go even further,

00:38:53.715 --> 00:38:56.351
and you can get quantitative.

00:38:56.351 --> 00:39:00.355
Because, you see, what we're
really talking about here

00:39:00.355 --> 00:39:03.858
is something called a dipole.

00:39:03.858 --> 00:39:06.361
That is why it's
called polar covalent.

00:39:08.930 --> 00:39:09.764
OK?

00:39:09.764 --> 00:39:13.568
It's called polar covalent
because we made a dipole.

00:39:13.568 --> 00:39:19.174
And as far as I know, from
my electricity and magnetism

00:39:19.174 --> 00:39:21.743
training, the dipole moment--

00:39:21.743 --> 00:39:23.778
mu, it's written as mu--

00:39:23.778 --> 00:39:25.880
is the charge
times the distance.

00:39:25.880 --> 00:39:28.616
Where Q is that the charge.

00:39:28.616 --> 00:39:31.753
In this case, it's literally
the charge that was pulled.

00:39:31.753 --> 00:39:33.688
It's exactly that.

00:39:33.688 --> 00:39:34.756
There's the chart, right?

00:39:34.756 --> 00:39:37.359
Tendency to pull
a shared electron.

00:39:39.961 --> 00:39:42.564
I pulled it over
here, so I created

00:39:42.564 --> 00:39:45.900
a little more
negative charge on me,

00:39:45.900 --> 00:39:48.436
and a little positive
charge on there.

00:39:48.436 --> 00:39:51.473
And that meant that I've
got a partial charge now,

00:39:51.473 --> 00:39:53.875
separated by a distance.

00:39:53.875 --> 00:39:54.376
Right?

00:39:54.376 --> 00:39:57.379
And so you can actually use
this to get quantitative.

00:39:57.379 --> 00:40:00.215
So let's do sodium
chloride, right?

00:40:00.215 --> 00:40:04.753
So for sodium chloride the
dipole moment is nine to debye.

00:40:04.753 --> 00:40:07.055
And this is just one debye.

00:40:07.055 --> 00:40:12.293
This is charge times distance,
so one debye is equal to 3.3.

00:40:12.293 --> 00:40:17.999
10 to the minus
30th coulomb meters.

00:40:17.999 --> 00:40:23.271
That's the units of a dipole
moment-- charge times distance.

00:40:23.271 --> 00:40:25.373
So if I take sodium
chloride and I tell you

00:40:25.373 --> 00:40:28.943
that its dipole
moment is nine debye,

00:40:28.943 --> 00:40:34.349
then I also have to tell you
that the distance between them,

00:40:34.349 --> 00:40:39.187
r, is equal to 2.36 angstroms.

00:40:39.187 --> 00:40:40.889
That is this distance here.

00:40:40.889 --> 00:40:45.059
I've made a bond between
these two things,

00:40:45.059 --> 00:40:49.030
and that bond is
some distance apart.

00:40:49.030 --> 00:40:50.698
Well, that's
exactly the distance

00:40:50.698 --> 00:40:52.534
I need to think about
the dipole, right?

00:40:52.534 --> 00:40:55.069
So remember, a dipole
is because I've

00:40:55.069 --> 00:40:58.206
got positive charge
and negative charge.

00:40:58.206 --> 00:41:01.409
And in fact, you might
see it written like this.

00:41:01.409 --> 00:41:04.679
You might see there's a
little positive charge here,

00:41:04.679 --> 00:41:06.581
and a little negative
charge there,

00:41:06.581 --> 00:41:09.818
and the dipole moment
goes like that.

00:41:09.818 --> 00:41:10.318
All right?

00:41:10.318 --> 00:41:11.186
There's a dipole.

00:41:15.490 --> 00:41:18.860
And now, those charges at
this distance in the bond

00:41:18.860 --> 00:41:21.629
allow me to actually
be quantitative,

00:41:21.629 --> 00:41:25.266
because Q is equal to mu over r.

00:41:25.266 --> 00:41:30.672
And if I plug that in,
then it's 1.3 times 10

00:41:30.672 --> 00:41:32.807
to the minus 19th coulombs.

00:41:32.807 --> 00:41:34.809
So for sodium chloride--

00:41:34.809 --> 00:41:38.713
because I was given the dipole
moment in that molecule,

00:41:38.713 --> 00:41:40.882
and I can look up
the bonds distance--

00:41:40.882 --> 00:41:45.987
I can actually tell you how
much charge are on those atoms

00:41:45.987 --> 00:41:48.923
if I know that information.

00:41:48.923 --> 00:41:54.529
But I also know what the
charge of a full electron is.

00:41:54.529 --> 00:41:56.331
This is almost there.

00:41:56.331 --> 00:42:07.075
It's something like
80% of an electron.

00:42:07.075 --> 00:42:12.013
And so that's a pretty
strong ionic bond.

00:42:12.013 --> 00:42:14.716
But it's not 100%, right?

00:42:14.716 --> 00:42:17.285
So this is the message
I want to tell you.

00:42:17.285 --> 00:42:20.455
In reality, we've been
talking about absolutes.

00:42:20.455 --> 00:42:22.957
I fully took a charge.

00:42:22.957 --> 00:42:23.725
Right?

00:42:23.725 --> 00:42:24.626
No!

00:42:24.626 --> 00:42:26.127
You didn't.

00:42:26.127 --> 00:42:28.062
You took 80%.

00:42:28.062 --> 00:42:30.298
And there's a little
bit still over there.

00:42:30.298 --> 00:42:30.798
Right?

00:42:33.301 --> 00:42:36.838
But we do like to still
categorize materials.

00:42:36.838 --> 00:42:38.506
And that's what this
does really nicely,

00:42:38.506 --> 00:42:41.776
is it says, well, this is going
to behave like an iconic solid.

00:42:41.776 --> 00:42:43.611
Meaning, it's going to
have those properties

00:42:43.611 --> 00:42:45.280
that I showed you on Monday.

00:42:45.280 --> 00:42:45.847
Right?

00:42:45.847 --> 00:42:50.652
Even though it's not fully
ionic, it's pretty darn ionic.

00:42:50.652 --> 00:42:51.452
Right?

00:42:51.452 --> 00:42:54.756
And now, even though these
are not fully covalent,

00:42:54.756 --> 00:42:59.027
meaning, pure, non-polar
covalent-- non-polar,

00:42:59.027 --> 00:43:02.230
no dipole in the bond.

00:43:02.230 --> 00:43:05.233
But these have some
dipole, but that's OK.

00:43:05.233 --> 00:43:09.170
They're still acting
like covalent molecules.

00:43:09.170 --> 00:43:10.238
Right?

00:43:10.238 --> 00:43:12.307
And so there's this
spectrum in between.

00:43:12.307 --> 00:43:15.843
And you can use these concepts
to think about the bonding,

00:43:15.843 --> 00:43:20.748
and to think about in particular
this-- that so many bonds are

00:43:20.748 --> 00:43:21.549
actually like this.

00:43:21.549 --> 00:43:23.251
Even though we categorize
them like this,

00:43:23.251 --> 00:43:25.820
and we think about
them like that,

00:43:25.820 --> 00:43:27.622
they actually look like this.

00:43:27.622 --> 00:43:32.493
And electronegativity
and dipoles

00:43:32.493 --> 00:43:37.398
are what help us characterize
these differences.

00:43:37.398 --> 00:43:44.138
Now, there is more to Lewis that
I will talk about on Friday.

00:43:44.138 --> 00:43:47.976
But there's something
called resonant structures,

00:43:47.976 --> 00:43:50.044
and that's a very
important part of Lewis.

00:43:50.044 --> 00:43:51.479
I'm going to talk
about it Friday,

00:43:51.479 --> 00:43:53.648
and it will not be on
the exam on Monday.

00:43:53.648 --> 00:43:54.148
OK?

00:43:54.148 --> 00:43:56.351
But on Friday, we'll talk
about resonance structures,

00:43:56.351 --> 00:43:57.919
we'll do a few more
Lewis examples,

00:43:57.919 --> 00:43:59.454
and then I'll talk
about exam one.

